	},
];

const mechanismLines = [
	'SIGNAL 1 · Global backpropagation',
	'  Standard cross-entropy over the full network.',
	'  Not modified. The global learning signal.',
	'',
	'SIGNAL 2 · The local loss (per-node health)',
	'  Each node: LayerNorm → Linear → PReLU → Linear',
	"  4,715 shared parameters across the whole network.",
	'',
	'SIGNAL 3 · Autonomous self-correction',
	'  When gate falls below threshold → one of eight diagnoses:',
	'    dead · saturated · oscillating · low_confidence',
	'    loss_stagnant · gradient_dead · unknown_sick · exploded',
	'  Each diagnosis routes to a diagnosis-specific treatment.',
	'  Every correction: mean-centred, clipped, logged.',
	'  Source: self_correction.py:190-197',
	'',
	'TELEMETRY (emitted natively, not reconstructed)',
	'  L1 · System health          global optimisation trajectory per epoch',
	'  L2 · Component conflict     per-node cosine similarity: global vs local gradient',
	'  L3 · Spatial routing        where the network is attending, per node',
	'  L4 · Mechanistic ablation   boolean tensor of every correction event',
	'  L5 · Parameter attribution  gradient of local loss through the bounded chain',
];

const gateEquation = katex.renderToString('g = \\max(0.3, g_{raw})', { throwOnError: false });

const citation = `@article{raadh2026ormas,
  title={ORMAS: Neural Architectural Transparency Enables Autonomous Self-Correction},
  author={Raadh, Rokib Al Dhin},
  year={2026},
  note={Submitted manuscript; under review. Not yet accepted.}
}`;

const evidenceStats =
	'383 experiments · 4 architecture families · 67 archived flight recorders\n16,316 lines · 85 Python files · Submitted manuscript';
---

<Page title="RESEARCH · SUBMITTED MANUSCRIPT">
	<section class="section container paper-header">
		<Eyebrow>RESEARCH · SUBMITTED MANUSCRIPT</Eyebrow>
		<h1>The Technology</h1>
		<div class="paper-meta">
			<p>Rokib Al Dhin Raadh · Independent Researcher</p>
			<p>Manuscript under review</p>
		</div>
		<p class="paper-intro">
			ORMAS is built on a single architectural decision: bounding each node's local gradient
			chain to four operations. That decision creates per-component addressability as a
			physical property of training.
		</p>
		<p class="paper-intro">
			The full technical foundation — the mechanism, the experiments, the results, the
			limitations — is documented in a submitted manuscript under review. Everything on this
			page is sourced to that manuscript. Every number has an error bar. Every claim has a
			condition.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">ABSTRACT</Eyebrow>
		<blockquote class="paper-abstract">{abstract}</blockquote>
		<p class="paper-positioning">
			"ORMAS is not an explainability layer. It is a training architecture that makes
			per-node causal attribution a physical property of the forward and backward pass —
			not a reconstruction after training is complete."
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">THE SIX RESULTS</Eyebrow>
		<ol class="results-ladder">
			{
				results.map((r) => (
					<li class="results-ladder-row">
						<p class="results-ladder-name">
							<span class="tabular">{String(r.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span> {r.name}
						</p>
						<p class="results-ladder-line">
							<strong>Condition:</strong> {r.condition}
						</p>
						<p class="results-ladder-line">
							<strong>Outcome:</strong> {r.outcome}
						</p>
						<p class="results-ladder-line">
							<strong>Baseline:</strong> {r.baseline}
						</p>
						<p class="results-ladder-gap tabular">{r.gap}</p>
						<p class="results-ladder-citation">{r.citation}</p>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">THE MECHANISM IN FIVE STEPS</Eyebrow>
		<TelemetryBlock label="Mechanism">{mechanismLines.join('\n')}</TelemetryBlock>
		<div class="paper-equation" set:html={gateEquation} />
		<p class="paper-equation-caption">Health gate — self_correction.py:190-197</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">WHAT WAS NOT TESTED</Eyebrow>
		<DisclosurePanel label="Named gaps in the evidence base">
			<p>
				Test-time distribution shift (CIFAR-10-C equivalents): not run. Named in the paper
				as a generalisation target.
			</p>
			<p>
				Adversarial weight injection: ORMAS is 1.0 pp worse than baseline. This is a known
				structural blind spot — adversarial weights maintain nominal activation statistics
				while degrading decision boundaries, evading exactly what ORMAS monitors. Published
				in the paper.
			</p>
			<p>
				Small-n viability: all results are on tens of thousands of examples. Whether the
				diagnostic signal survives at n ≈ 30 is untested. Load-bearing for the biotech
				market segment.
			</p>
			<p>
				Transformer-head behaviour: specified in the paper as a generalisation target. The
				monitoring half is cheap. The correction half has not been implemented.
			</p>
		</DisclosurePanel>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">SCOPE STATEMENT AND EVIDENCE BASE</Eyebrow>
		<p class="paper-scope">
			We do not claim that ORMAS achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on clean benchmarks, nor
			that it replaces specialized noisy-label algorithms. We claim that a single-network
			architecture can achieve competitive accuracy while providing mechanistically logged,
			per-node causal attribution that no prior single-network method offers — and that this
			structural transparency is what enables autonomous recovery from failures that standard
			architectures cannot detect.
		</p>
		<p class="paper-scope paper-scope-emphasis">
			The claim is transparency; the accuracy gap is its evidence.
		</p>
		<TelemetryBlock>{evidenceStats}</TelemetryBlock>
		<p class="paper-scope">
			The 67 archived flight recorders are drawn from the full 383-experiment programme. All
			383 runs are reproducible from seed. The submission includes a reproducibility
			checklist. The repository will be made public after the review period ends.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">CITATION</Eyebrow>
		<TelemetryBlock>{citation}</TelemetryBlock>
	</section>
</Page>
